rible to start shutting down schools  and businesses again but uh some light  touch policies like masking may need to  be reproduced well into the future if  things don't go the way we want it to go  i want to get more into that in a moment  but as we sort of approach this next  step and and i sense caution in your  tone a little bit but at the same time  are we at the point now when we are just  frankly dealing with a different kind of  threat and that we need to see public  health through a wider lens  we absolutely do i mean we absolutely do  it's it's extremely important to  recognize not just a singular focus on  the virus but of course all other other  facets of society but you can't ignore  the virus right we can't bury our head  in the sand and pretend this doesn't  exist right it's not every day that you  know 25 to 30 percent of your hospital  admissions are related to one single  cause so i think it's extremely  important that we obviously not ignore  what can be a significant public health  threat and which has been a significant  public health threat but also  acknowledge that we do have the tools to  keep this at bay and to prevent this  from impacting us here in canada as  significantly as it has i listen we're  walking a bit of a tight rope now  between pandemic to what we might  consider as endemic we're in this  transitionary period i think we'll see  some you know turbulence along the way  but eventually we'll get there  now the good and the bad of the last  phase if you will with omicron is that  the numbers  went sky high in a lot of cases as we  saw in many other countries as well so  whipped through this country many ways  but then there was a sharp drop it there  wasn't while there's a strain on the  health care system there's no question  it didn't overwhelm it at least you know  in comparison but given that and i want  to ask you of the power of  infection recovered immunity  otherwise known as natural immunity how  important is that  given